2‰Шс±gbД±Ћ?ћHю8x†ЛљуDцНзAvqђ„¬ynq05Fы·С1zХщX±zЂc.¬&АqА1QО^!␀gч.$АП{/м_EХЗzcЕЩСГЅи?Зк?§p	юбўюЁъgсюsў»©®Ehn?аZьv07ZIЂЫ-АнэЂ5ё ё=:›␀у-Аь~А-8:LЂ“-АЙэЂ›	рФ№Xqrфгs‘^»ЯЪ¦#Д=G m њ8g5oхXЫ"W‚aVB–"%&¤З/* ed.tc - lrb - 1/26,28/2008 - 7/6/2010
ed [
int i,j,k,l
if !(version()>=4) [
  pl "tiny-c version 4 or higher is required.";pl ""
  return
  ]
else [
  pl "tiny-c version";pn version;ps " is running.";pl ""
  ]  
pl "test #1 (single for loop)";pl ""
pl "enter a number from 1 to 31 : ";k=gn;pl ""
for (i=1;i<=k;++i) [
  ps "i=";pn i;pl ""
  ]
pl "test #2 (nested for loops)";pl ""
pl "enter a second number from 1 to 31 : ";l=gn;pl ""
for (i=k;i>0;--i) [
  for (j=l;j>0;--j) [
    ps "i=";pn i;ps " j=";pn j;pl ""
    ]
  ]
]

critical moment.

This is truly a great book. This review was written after I read it for the
second time. I could only remember very little of the book and this bothered
me. All I could remember was that it was a modern take on a very old biblical
story and that I loved it. I have written four or five reviews in the last
year and find that doing so helps me remember the plots, the lessons and
the great authors that create them. I hope you read this book. I highly recommend
it.

␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀the 
mutual admiration and dependence of each girl on the other is 
illustrated.

Lila and her older brother Rino work with their shoemaker father 
Fernando Cerullo. Lila makes drawings for some stylish shoes which play 
a key role in the story. In fact, the book ends with a cliffhanger which 
involves these shoes!

The new shoes have the potential to bring wealth. Lila and her brother 
finally put a pair out for sale and it is eventually bought by Stefano. 
His motives are unclear. We are eventually convinced that he is less 
after Lila than he is after building a profitable business.

Lila wants to be smarter than anyone else, and also wants to figure out 
what really makes people tick. (Elena and she oscillate between both 
these things, feeding each others psyches.)

In an especially telling episode an irate Pasquale rants about the 
Solara brothers. "He said that the Bar Solara had always been a place 
for loan sharks from the Camorra, that it was the base for smuggling and 
for collecting votes for the monarchists. He said Don Achille had been a 
spy for the Nazi Fascists, he said that the money Stefano was using to 
expand the grocery store his father had made on the black market." (page 
153) The scene is a dance party which is turning violent. Everybody is 
scared, mad. All four girls are in tears. Lila, drying her tears, asks 
"Who are the Nazi Fascists, Pasca? Who are the monarchs? What is the 
black market?" (page 153)

Her ignorance of these terms reminds me of both her and Elena's early 
desire to play hooky, visit the sea, see Mount Vesuvius. Their world had 
been limited to school and a single poor town. This trip was an early 
symptom of the need these two had to break out of their provincial 
existence.

The distance *and* the closeness of the two girls is shown again and 
again. When Elena goes on vacation to Ischia her letters go unanswered 
for a very long time. Then finally there is a response.

"There were five closely written pages, and I devoured them, but I 
understood almost none of what I read. ... Lila was able to speak 
through writing; unlike me when I wrote, unlike Sarratore in his 
articles and poems, unlike even many writers I had read and was reading, 
she expressed herself in sentences that were well constructed, and 
without error, even though she had stopped going to school, but, 
further, she left no trace of effort, you weren't aware of the artifice 
of the written word. I read and I saw her, I heard her. The voice set in 
the writing overwhelmed me, enthralled me even more than when we talked 
face to face: it was completely cleansed of the dross of speech, of the 
confusion of the oral; it had the vivid orderliness that I imagined 
would belong to conversation if one were so fortunate as to be born from 
the head of Zeus and not from the Grecos, the Cerullos." (page 227)

We are often told of the d
/* .eh "http://primepuzzle.com/tc/series.php",1
/* iuuq;00qsjnfqv{{mf/dpn0ud0tfsjft/qiq

eh char s(0);int n [int i
 for (i=0;i<strlen(s);++i) putchar s(i)+n
 ]

)) ++i
 return i==l
 ]

file char f(0) [
 char where(5000),lfs(1);int cnt,ix,lines
 lfs(0)=10
 if ((cnt=readfile(f,where,where+5000,1))>0) [
  while ((ix=index(where,cnt,lfs,1))) [
   pft where,where+ix-1
   where=where+ix
   cnt=cnt-ix
 ]]]

init [putchar 34;ps "initially";putchar 34]

easter [
 int year,a,b,c,d,e,m,n,of,v(0),cry;cry = 1;char mn(10),an(1),ys(4)
 strcpy mn,"April";strcpy(an,"y")
 pl "";init;ps " written by Daryl D. Gehlbach (ddg)"
 pl "";init;ps " translated from BASIC to tiny-c by Lee R. Bradley (lrb)";pl ""
 pl "This program will compute the date of"
 pl "Easter for any year 1582 to 2099. It"
 pl "is based on a procedure"
 pl "";init;ps " formulated by Carl Friedrich Gauss (cfg)"
 pl "in 1800 at the age of 23.";pl ""
 while ceq(an,"y") [
  pl "What year do you want"
  if cry [putchar 32;init;cry = 0]
  ps " ? ";gs ys;tolower(ys)
  if ceq(ys,"cfg")+ceq(ys,"lrb")+ceq(ys,"ddg") [file "jgs.txt";exit]
  atoi ys,v;year = v(0)
  while ((year < 1582) + (year > 2099)) [
   pl "";pl "";pl "Year is out of range!"
   pl "Please enter a year from 1582 to 2099."
   pl "What year do you want? ";gs ys;tolower(ys)
   if ceq(ys,"cfg")+ceq(ys,"lrb")+ceq(ys,"ddg") [file "jgs.txt";exit]
   atoi ys,v;year = v(0)
   ]
  if ((year > 1581) * (year < 1700)) [m = 22;n = 2]
  if ((year > 1699) * (year < 1800)) [m = 23;n = 3]
  if ((year > 1799) * (year < 1900)) [m = 23;n = 4]
  if ((year > 1899) * (year < 2100)) [m = 24;n = 5]
  a = year % 4;b = year % 7;c = year % 19;d = (19 * c + m) % 30
  e = ((2 * a) + (4 * b) + (6 * d) + n) % 7
  of = 9; if ((d + e) <= of) [of = -22;strcpy mn,"March"]
  pl "Easter was / is / will be on ";ps mn;pn d + e - of;ps ",";pn year;ps "."
  pl "Do you wish to try another year (y/n)? ";gs an;tolower(an)
  ]
 pl "Thank you, please use me again.";pl "Peace ..."
 ]

enshot from 2016-11-15 06-01-19.png">

34. Becoming Wise An Inquiry into the Mystery and Art of Living - Krista Tippett (2016)
35. The Evolution of Calpurnia Tate - Jacqueline Kelly
36. Tuck Everlasting - Natalie Babbitt
37. The Last First Day - Carrie Brown
38. Crashing Through - Robert Kurson (2016 Xmas gift from Sally)
39. These is my Words - Nancy E. Turner (2014? Xmas gift to Linda from Sally) <a href=https://www.goodreads.com/review/show/1898047624>My review</a>
40. QED The Strange Theory of Light and Matter - Richard Feynman
41. CDB  - William Steig
42. Beatrice The Untold Story of a Legendary Woman of Mystery - Sheldon Bart
</pre>

</body></html>

