/* mod.tc - 11/18/14 - lrb */

mod [
 int a,b,c,d,x,y
 ps "a? ";a=gn
 ps "b? ";b=gn
 ps "c? ";c=gn
 ps "d? ";d=gn
 x=( a*( (b+c)%d ) )%d
 y=( (a*b)%d + (a*c)%d )%d
 pn x==y
]

 of one Jim Williams; Part One covers the six or so main
characters and Part Two the trials themselves.

The book is terribly funny on many occasions, especially in the scenes that
involve Chablis, a black drag queen ("The Empress of Savannah") and Minerva,
a "voodoo priestess who works her magic in the graveyard at midnight."

Berendt spends a lot of time on the architecture and history of Savannah
and the sometimes very nutty people that live there during the time this
story takes place (1970s and 1980s).

Joe Odum, a piano player, is introduced early, engaged in stealing electricity
from the house next door to power his place which we soon learn is frequented
and often lived in by a myriad of locals. The author / narrator's relationship
with Joe is that of a confidant for want of a better word. Berendt tells
Joe he is writing a book and Joe describes himself to him on page 257 (of
388) as the "good guy" compared to all the others he's hooked him up with
for his story. (By this time we know Joe writes bad checks, lives and entertains
in houses that aren't his etc.) At a St. Patrick's Day Parade, upon seeing
a float carrying a motionless blue-clad Union soldier, Joe responds to our
narrator's comment "The Civil War's been over for quite a while. Isn't it
time all that was forgotten?" as follows:

"Not if you're a southerner. But you know, that dead Yankee, isn't just about
the Civil War. He's sort of a symbol of what could happen to any Yankee,
even a modern-day Yankee, who comes down here and gets folks all riled up.
He could be some fellow from New York [Berendt is from New York] who decided
to write a book about us and started filling it with drag queens and murderers
and corpses and bottles of poison and -- what's that you were telling me
just a minute ago? Oh yeah, voodoo! Voodoo! Witchcraft in a graveyard! Damn!"

"I'm not making any of this up, Joe," I said.

"I'm not saying you are."

"So I take it you don't really disapprove."

"No. As a matter of fact, when I think about it, it suits me fine. See, with
all these weirdos you got filling up your book, I figure somebody's gonna
have to play the good guy, and it looks like it'll be me."

"In Cold Blood" and "Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil" share excellence
in investigative reporting. But "Midnight" is funny, Capote is not. On the
serious side of "Midnight," the bungled police job, the rivalries of defense
and prosecution attorneys and politicians and the nonchalance of Joe Williams,
the defendant, during the trials (he continues to buy expensive antiques,
routing his calls to Europe through the house he has turned into a bogus museum
while he's in jail etc. etc.) all contribute to this thrilling story.

I highly recommend this book.

