 MГUҐ©Ѓ5¤&¤Jњ'!NY„j8BDбBd‚*ЋЎ©ЃAf©aHDEJl ©к$C–aZГDРНкIФ2«jђЂGЈЎjY®.µЎЕЪлєфT±‚NЙмЗ␀DћD ИHB␀Q"Э·’Ў2–DjE*.:ш3Y…K$MPPлA„–­­kдФД:o‘‰ЁOyBBHG¦EТЗ)І‘бeOGT…»Ж!X^2kFЧИ{ЯО9ш+ђQЪ=ЬFо:W1Р-}ЖeНЂ[Ёmй¤“њf2RеІ}!—IfPµєU–”a’Жм<nnЯ­FS!§M™ЯF¬e•BЬhF1ўFёЗG™а^Уf8'tѓ6|ЌУ!”ЪeН»ЋqўhcЙЖ]BaМFьтBtќGZ@vЭЧ}ОзџЙ»Ћ}QЌ‡p¶gИ†ЗњoЌ·Ўf~ЎP^&ЛТ 8зtЖ¶о„‡(PSµхLeИџЭ—ФeнВдyҐ(Љ$"h  ~І'њY¶ЦРa!-ЩчХMУ(ШЭ‚ё„rк™ aЅ¦“РТ‹i‡eЧ7ДђOA␀s%␀кµ¤уО:LЌҐЏ›"аЩ␀RXzA
This is a modern version of the Cain and Abel story. Cal and Aron are the
sons of Adam Trask (both boys have the same mother but the identity of the
father is a little murky; Adam's half-brother Charles may have fathered one
of them). In some ways this uncertainty is irrelevant but in others it is
key. The love or preference of a father of one son over another plays an
important role in the book.

The time period is roughly 1862 to 1918. Connecticut and California are the
stage. Adam's father has a brief stint in the Union army and is wounded.
He ends up creating an entirely fictitious story of his service record, claiming
involvement in many important battles. His son Adam is forced to join the
army by him and even reenlists even though he does not want to be a soldier,
bored with civilian life. Adam finally leaves the army and spends about 5
years bumming all over the country. Adam's brother Charles anxiously awaits
his return despite the fact that they had several serious physical fights
before he left (all caused by Charles' feeling his father preferred Adam).

A parallel story takes place in the Salinas Valley where we are told of the
Hamilton family. Irish immigrants, Samuel Hamilton and his wife Liza have
many children. Will is ambitious and clever. Of the 8 others, who all end
up playing roles in the story, the youngest girl is Olive who is the author's
mother. This establishes the connection Steinbeck has to the characters in
his book. We will return to them shortly.

We are introduced to Cathy Ames and learn of her evil streak . She is caught
by her parents seducing someone at the age of 10, turns away from a teacher
/ lover who then commits suicide, and kills her parents by burning the house
down. Later, Cathy shows up badly injured at the Trask family doorstep, beaten
by a whoremaster she worked for, and is nurtured back to life by Adam.

Adam marries Cathy and continues to create a completely false image of where
Cathy is coming from. By this time we know Charles knows Cathy's past and
has slept with her. Adam and Charles find they have inherited a great deal
of money when their father dies and despite the fact that Cathy does not
want to they leave Charles behind and move to California to start a life
of farming. The source of the inheritance is troublesome to the brothers.
Where did their father get the money? 

The two children mentioned at the beginning of this review are born. Samuel
Hamilton was present and essentially forced Cathy to give birth. They were
unwanted by Cathy who tried to abort them. Soon after Cathy tells Adam she
is leaving and just before doing so, she shoots Adam. He does not die.

A Chinese servant, Lee, is hired to take care of the children who remain
unnamed for a long time. Hamilton, who knows about water and how to find
it helps Adam who needs water for the farm. He also tries to get Adam out
of his depressed state and resorts to knocking him out in a fight to get
him to name his kids.

Cathy changes her name to Kate and returns to whoring. She ends up poisoning
the madam and takes over the whorehouse.

This tragic tale continues as Aron and Cal grow up. They meet Abra when they
are about 16. Abra is a bit like Cal who in turn is a bit like his mother
(whom he does not know anything about as he has been told she is dead). Not
unlike Adam and Charles, Aron and Cal are loved and not loved by their father
causing rivalry and hatred between the two. Through rumors and  eavesdropping
Cal learns his mother is alive and what she is. Much later he forces his
brother to meet her, somehow unable to control his meanness. Aron falls apart
and enlists in the army to fight in WW I (recalling his father's army stint).

An extremely important theme is introduced early in the book when Lee explains
to us what his teachers finally conclude is the key idea of the sixteen verses
of the fourth chapter of Genesis (Cain and Abel). 

"Lee's hand shook as he filled the delicate cups. He drank his down in one
gulp. "Don't you see?" he cried. "The American Standard translation orders
men to triumph over sin, and you can call sin ignorance. The King James translation
makes a promise in 'Thou shalt,' meaning that men will surely triumph over
sin. But the Hebrew word, the word timshel 'Thou mayest' that gives a choice.
It might be the most important word in the world. That says the way is open.
That throws it right back on a man. For if 'Thou mayest' it is also true
that 'Thou mayest not.' Don't you see?" 

I have of course had to skim over many many details and will conclude by
not telling you the ending. I will only say that Timshel is uttered by Adam
at a critical moment.

This is truly a great book. This review was written after I read it for the
second time. I could only remember very little of the book and this bothered
me. All I could remember was that it was a modern take on a very old biblical
story and that I loved it. I have written four or five reviews in the last
year and find that doing so helps me remember the plots, the lessons and
the great authors that create them. I hope you read this book. I highly recommend
it.

