We learn in the Prologue that we are about to read the author's 
recollections of a roughly fifty year period. The end of the period is 
about 2010, Lila and every trace of her are gone. The beginning of the 
period is about 1958. The Childhood and Adolescence of Lila and Elena 
form the next 300 pages. The place is Naples, Italy.

This book is the first of a four book series, The Neapolitan Novels. 
Although a pseudonym, Ferrante shares a first name with her narrator, 
Elena Greco. Autobiographical or not, the realism of the author's 
description of the players, places and plights are striking.

An index of the characters is provided. Nine families are listed, each 
with six or seven members. Some have multiple names. It's OK if you lose 
track of people on occasion!

A single thread binds this tale together: two young girls are in some 
sense a single soul. The title, "My Brilliant Friend," is assumed to 
mean Elena's description of Lila but very late in the book we hear Lila 
say to Elena "Not for you: you're my brilliant friend, you have to be 
the best of all, boys and girls." (page 312) Throughout the book, the 
mutual admiration and dependence of each girl on the other is 
illustrated.

Lila and her older brother Rino work with their shoemaker father 
Fernando Cerullo. Lila makes drawings for some stylish shoes which play 
a key role in the story. In fact, the book ends with a cliffhanger which 
involves these shoes!

The new shoes have the potential to bring wealth. Lila and her brother 
finally put a pair out for sale and it is eventually bought by Stefano. 
His motives are unclear. We are eventually convinced that he is less 
after Lila than he is after building a profitable business.

Lila wants to be smarter than anyone else, and also wants to figure out 
what really makes people tick. (Elena and she oscillate between both 
these things, feeding each others psyches.)

In an especially telling episode an irate Pasquale rants about the 
Solara brothers. "He said that the Bar Solara had always been a place 
for loan sharks from the Camorra, that it was the base for smuggling and 
for collecting votes for the monarchists. He said Don Achille had been a 
spy for the Nazi Fascists, he said that the money Stefano was using to 
expand the grocery store his father had made on the black market." (page 
153) The scene is a dance party which is turning violent. Everybody is 
scared, mad. All four girls are in tears. Lila, drying her tears, asks 
"Who are the Nazi Fascists, Pasca? Who are the monarchs? What is the 
black market?" (page 153)

Her ignorance of these terms reminds me of both her and Elena's early 
desire to play hooky, visit the sea, see Mount Vesuvius. Their world had 
been limited to school and a single poor town. This trip was an early 
symptom of the need these two had to break out of their provincial 
existence.

The distance *and* the closeness of the two girls is shown again and 
again. When Elena goes on vacation to Ischia her letters go unanswered 
for a very long time. Then finally there is a response.

"There were five closely written pages, and I devoured them, but I 
understood almost none of what I read. ... Lila was able to speak 
through writing; unlike me when I wrote, unlike Sarratore in his 
articles and poems, unlike even many writers I had read and was reading, 
she expressed herself in sentences that were well constructed, and 
without error, even though she had stopped going to school, but, 
further, she left no trace of effort, you weren't aware of the artifice 
of the written word. I read and I saw her, I heard her. The voice set in 
the writing overwhelmed me, enthralled me even more than when we talked 
face to face: it was completely cleansed of the dross of speech, of the 
confusion of the oral; it had the vivid orderliness that I imagined 
would belong to conversation if one were so fortunate as to be born from 
the head of Zeus and not from the Grecos, the Cerullos." (page 227)

We are often told of the disconnect between Elena's academic success 
(Latin, Greek, Mathematics) and Lila's shoemaking, between Lila's often 
risky lifestyle and Elena's reserved demeanor. Elena is bookish, Lila is 
bold. Elena worries that Lila is drifting away, becoming stuffy, 
"practical," manipulative.

What happens in "The Story of a New Name," the second book in the 
series, is anybody's guess. I cannot wait to find out!

tes the sum of a and b and puts it in a. */

add int a(0),b(0),d [
 int i,c,w
 for (i=0;i<=d;++i) [
  w=a(d-i)+b(d-i)+c;a(d-i)=w%10;c=w/10
 ]]

/* multiply computes the product of m and a and puts it in a. */

multiply int a(0),m,d [
 int i,c,w
 for (i=0;i<=d;++i) [
  w=a(d-i)*m+c;a(d-i)=w%10;c=w/10
 ]]

main [
 pl "multseq.tc - 9/14/13 - tct"
 int a(6000),b(6000),i,j,d,n
 pl "enter a number less than 12000: ";n=gn;pl ""
 d=(n+1)/2;a(d)=3
 for (i=0;i<=n-2;++i) [ /* compute 3^n */
  putchar '.';if ((i+1)%40==0) pl ""
  multiply(a,3,d)
  ]
 for (i=0;n>0;++i) [b(d-i)=n%10;n=n/10]
 multiply(b,3,d) /* b now holds 3n */
 subtract(a,b,d) /* compute 3^n-3n */
 i=0;while (a(i)==0) ++i /* don't print leading 0's */
 j=i;pl "";pl "The";pn d-i+1;ps " digit value is";pl "";putchar(7);sak;pl "";pl ""
 while i<=d [
  MC a(i),14
  ++i;if ((i-j)%40==0) pl ""
  ]
 pl ""
 ]

