~Н+#щЙН5%noname␀ЙНН(% Named Directory Data ␀Нг( -Н(%Named Directory Buffer Not Available␀ГмнSБ+Н¶$␀~·Кмє(W{жД¶$␀Н¶$~Ж@Н+#~Нѓ*Н5%: ␀#~Н+#щ	Н5%  ␀{жМ¶$їНН(% Flow Command Package ␀НЖ( -Н(%Flow Command Package not implemen
Cider House Rules

This is my third John Irving. It is a story about a fellow who runs an 
"orphanage." I quote the word because the orphanage at St. Cloud's is not 
your standard orphanage; Wilbur Larch performs abortions and deliveries 
as well as receive and place orphans.

Homer Wells and he are the main characters in the book. Homer is an 
orphan who is difficult to place and who eventually becomes a permanent 
resident and "employee." He eventually does leave, breaking a promise he 
made to his "girlfriend" Melony (also an orphan) that he wouldn't 
without her. He works at Ocean View, an apple orchard, and becomes as 
good at all the kinds of work that go on there as he was at helping Larch.

There is an incredible amount of detail on both obstetrics and cider 
production in the book. The ethics of abortion is a key issue for Homer 
while he attends them and much, much later in the book but I must let 
you read the final chapters to understand what I mean here!

Melony leaves the orphanage too and spends a lot of time searching for 
Homer. His departure from St Cloud's was only to be for a few days but 
ends up longterm. He loves the farm life and falls in love with the 
daughter of the owners of the farm, Candy. She becomes pregnant by 
Wally, her husband to be, and has an abortion at St Cloud's (she and 
Wally are not married). Wally goes off to war and eventually Homer and 
Candy have a baby whom they name Angel. To keep the relationship secret, 
they arrange to convince everyone that Angel is an adopted son of 
Homer's. Wally is shot down and eventually presumed dead but much later 
returns home, paralyzed. Candy and Wally marry. The truth about Angel's 
parentage weighs on Candy and Homer and when to tell both the son and 
Wally is difficult.

Where does the book get its title from? Rules of what is allowed and 
disallowed in the cider house at Ocean View are posted at the mill. They 
are regularly disobeyed. The whole idea that there are two sets of rules 
is a key idea of the book. What those that work the farm, both white and 
black, actually do and what is permitted differ dramatically. "Mr Rose," 
the head of the migrant crew, and Homer challenge each other on this 
point. Again, I won't explain any more than this.

Irving's "Garp" and his "Rules" share a few things in common: in both, 
New England and the South are featured, Blacks and Whites and their 
relationship are central, and, finally, who actually murders whom is ... 
I'll stop there.

I highly recommend this book.

