НсcЙ␀␀␀␀␀"™Ї2*N␀"›™Н›НВ*\~ю]Кю;КюКю/К:H·А*›е*™е*еН¶б}б"™б"›<2ќНВХ
I learned of this book from a friend. I had never heard of Isabel Allende
but recognized the last name and now know the former President of Chile was
her Dad's cousin. I have recently read several books that mix the magical,
the allegorical, and the real and perhaps my friend pointed me toward "The
House of the Spirits" because she has been reading my reviews and knows what
I like.

The book is a fictional account of three generations and a century of Chile's
tumultuous past, culminating in the 1973 coup. It was published in 1983.
The main character, Esteban Trueba, is 90 when the book ends.

Right from the start the reader is easily swept away by the author's often
long and at times mesmerizing sentences. When Isabel received word of her
grandfather's imminent death she wrote him a letter and thus began her plan
to keep him and what he was alive for those who would follow.

I learned a lot about Chile from this book. My brother-in-law has mentioned
Henry Kissinger in less than glowing terms in the past and this struck me
as odd since I had found him a skilled negotiator. But the complicity of
Nixon and Kissinger in dislodging the democratically elected Marxist Allende
and bringing the dictator Pinochet into power is no longer a secret.

The abject poverty and suffering of the underclass and the scourge of natural
disasters and lawless rule fill the pages. The death of Rosa the Beautiful,
Trueba's first love and his subsequent marriage to Clara the Clairvoyant
(her sister) are our first glimpse into the disparity of the caring women
and the often violent men of the time. Clara's daughter Blanca and, later,
granddaughter, Alba, repeat the main theme of the book; their wisdom is revealed
through the guise of predictive powers. Each of the women is associated with
either forced relationships with men or lovers unacceptable in the eyes of
others (or both!). A truly evil individual tortures Alba and only through
Senator Trueba's plea to Transito Soto, a whore who he is sure must know
her whereabouts, is she saved. Trueba finally realizes that what he has believed,
promoted and done his whole life is what has helped bring about disaster
to his country and he desperately wants to save his granddaughter, the only
love he has left.

Perhaps this positive note is a predictive take on later chapters in Chile's
history. Pinochet is gone. President Ricardo Lagos Escobar is elected by unhindered
ballot in 2000.

I highly recommend this book.

