/* type.tc - tct - 5/29,30/9 6/1/9 3/22/10 */

/* displays a file */

type char f(0); [
 pl "";ps "type.tc - 3/22/10";pl "";pl "";
 char where(15000),lfs(1);int cnt,ix,lines;
 lfs(0)=10; /* ascii code for linefeed */
 if ((cnt=readfile(f,where,where+15000,1))>0) [ /* read file into memory */
  while ((ix=index(where,cnt,lfs,1))>0) [ /* loop while linefeeds are detected */
   pft where,where+ix-1; /* print a line */
   where=where+ix; /* next search will start at the beginning of the next line */
   cnt=cnt-ix; /* decrement the count of characters to search */
   if (++lines>19) [sak;pl "";pl "";lines=0;] /* force user to hit Enter every 20 lines */
 ]]]

chool and educate the common 
men and women of the area.

The two lesser players, Digger Venn (the "reddleman") and Clym's mother, 
have goals of their own. He loves Eustacia too and Clym's mother is set 
against her son's attraction to Eustacia.

A key device Hardy repeatedly uses in this fateful tale is to cause the 
various players to misunderstand the intentions of each other. This he 
does through unread letters, unopened doors, and the like. It is only 
through chance correspondence of the misunderstood and / or misinformed 
that what really is true becomes known.

It seems none of the principals in this story can make up her / his mind 
what she / he truly desires; Eustacia wants to go to Paris and live the 
life her husband Clym has decided to reject but she also allows both men 
who love her to care for her. Wildeve too is duplicitous, finally 
marrying one but longing for another. Clym is entranced by Eustacia yet 
buries himself in books to prepare himself for his schoolmaster goal.

A review would not be complete without mentioning Hardy's mastery of the 
English language: his sentences are often long, incredibly descriptive 
(of peoples' deepest emotions and secrets and of the heath itself) and 
contrasts markedly from my previously reviewed Follett who is a great 
story teller but does not have Hardy's language skills.

How this tale ends is of course for you to find out; like all great 
fiction, you learn very late. The conclusion is a convincing and 
positive one.

I highly recommend this book!

