Hi Tom,

Need a *real* computer and *real* editor to respond to your question. By 
which I mean not my cell phone or its editor ... so using my "mainframe" 
and gedit, the default editor under Ubuntu.

I don't miss Windows at all. In fact it's nothing but a joy not to have 
to put up w/ it for now I think 4 or so months.

As I am sure you know, Ubuntu's front end, its GUI, is not unlike 
Windows in many ways. So it's linux for sure but the wonderfuly arcane 
command suite (ls, rm, cp, mv, cd, chmod etc etc) are seldom used 
(unless your name is Bradley, in which case terminal mode is your 
favorite state).

I have found that *every* can't-do-without application that I required 
in my daily use under Windows has its counterpart under Ubuntu. 
Screenprint, a good editor, sftp, disc burning, a graphics editor, 
PuTTY, email client and web browser (Thunderbird / Firefox), C compiler, 
application update facility, file manager etc. And ... every one of them 
is far superior to the ones I used under the other operating system.

I *never* crash. *Never* can't connect to the internet.

I can quickly go into terminal mode against both the local computer and 
remote server.

I no longer use a laptop but if I did I'd toss Windows there too. I do 
have a tablet and its Android op sys is linux driven and awesome.

In summary, my "conversion" was instantaneous when it finally occurred. 
Very little learning curve (although it did help to have a pretty good 
knowledge of linux to begin with). Linux over Windows is not unlike CP/M 
over DOS: way better and always has been. Also way less popular (but 
that's slowly changing I think). It's amazing how often something that's 
better is not a commercial winner.

Thanks for asking. Nice to hear from you!

Lee

